WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.390
Hey Thoughty2 here
With the wide reach of the internet, we’ve

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.400
seen how fast a meme can spread and then,
before you know it, they’re gone again like

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.780
a ghost in the night .
But this also happened before the internet

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:25.009
too. Let’s have a look at some crazy crazes
and fabulous fads that took the world by storm.

00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:30.239
Now in the modern world of consumerism, we
all buy plenty of stuff that we don’t need.

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:35.579
In fact, that’s probably most of what we
buy. This means that selling us useless products

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:41.179
has become something of an art form and led
some people to wonder; what won’t people

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:45.649
pay for?
In 1975, Gary Dahl, gave a pretty convincing

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:53.699
case that you really can sell absolutely anything.
For just under $4 he gave you a smooth rock,

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:58.159
one you could have just picked up off the
beach or a river bank.

00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:03.510
Pet Rocks were probably the biggest craze
of the year and although it died out within

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:09.000
about 6 months, it was enough time for Gary
to become a millionaire. Your rock came in

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.970
a little box, with a bed of straw and a 32-page
user guide, explaining the difficult task

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.329
of feeding, grooming and training your new
best friend.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:22.799
He hit on the idea when he heard his buddies
talking in a bar, complaining about having

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:28.439
to take care of their pets; all that food
and poop and walks and eating your shoes and

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:33.380
making your house hairier than a barbershop’s
floor. So what if you could avoid all these

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:39.030
irritating issues and have a simple pet that
loved you, only the way inanimate granite

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:45.119
can love? A rock is cleaner than a dog, quieter
than a hamster, probably friendlier than a

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:50.009
cat and at least as intelligent as a goldfish.
It’s a win win.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:57.189
Gary sadly died in 2015 but he wanted everyone
to share in his super sales powers. In 2001

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:03.689
he wrote the famous; advertising for dummies.
Who the dummies were in this situation, was

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:08.420
not exactly clear.
Whether it’s rocks or cats, pets are hugely

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:15.160
popular and it seems like our love of cute
animals is unlimited. But trust Japan to take

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.820
it a little too far.
Kigurumi are the animal costumed performers

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:25.540
that you see at many promotional events, sports
shows and so on. But the cutest army in the

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.900
world burst onto the streets in the mid 2000s,
seen getting on with their daily lives dressed

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.440
in a range of cartoon onesies.
Japan has been obsessed with cuteness for

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:43.210
a long time and their term “Kawaii” can
refer to everything from clothing to shows

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:49.310
to behaviour, even food; who doesn’t want
a sandwich with an adorable face, right?

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:54.940
Many trends have grown out of this, from the
creepily named fashion of “Sweet Lolita”,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:03:00.280
which consist of lacy bonnets, bows and umbrellas,
as well as whitening make-up, to things like

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:06.220
Hello Kitty and burikko ji, a style of fake
child-like handwriting.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:12.680
The most popular Kigurumin outfit was probably
the little, yellow, rodent Pokemon; Pikachu,

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.610
partly because of the character’s mass appeal
but also because the bright yellow colour

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.260
masked a lot of the mustard and tea stains
that your daily onesie wearing normally creates.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:26.670
The mighty power of cuteness could not be
contained to Japan for very long. Soon, students

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:32.270
across the UK and America were nursing their
hangovers in furry style. It’s hard to feel

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:37.320
sorry for yourself when you are a unicorn;
I think that has been proven by science.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.870
Speaking of science, our next craze shows
just how quickly our understanding of something

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.270
can change.
In 1898, Marie Curie and her husband Pierre

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:53.680
discovered Radium, element number 88 in the
periodic table. It’s a metal and has a half-life

00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:01.620
of between 3.5 days and 1600 years, depending
on which isotope you’re dealing with.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.920
For those of you who aren’t sure; let me
quickly explain the basics of what a “half-life”

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:12.320
is. If you have an amount of a substance which
is radioactive, then its atoms are not completely

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:19.169
stable. So, they sometimes decay and send
out particles of matter (alpha and beta) or

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:25.870
energy (in the form of gamma rays). The half-life
is a prediction of how long it will take before

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:32.150
half of the atoms in your lump of stuff have
decayed. It’s really more of a probability.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:37.430
The crazy thing is; if you had a piece of
Radium just the size of a grain of sand, with

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:44.110
a half-life of 1600 years, it would still
be decaying at a rate of around half a billion

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:49.120
atoms a second!
So, back to the craze. When it was announced

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.900
that Radium could be used to attack cancer
cells, people went Radium crazy and it became

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.680
a health fad. They put it in lotions, syrups,
tablets and plenty of other stuff. It was

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:06.120
basically the Kale of the early 20th century.
Unfortunately, while Kale is just a simple

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:12.310
leaf, Radium makes you sick to your bones,
since it often causes cancer there. Marie

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:17.430
Currie herself died of a cancer to her bone
marrow and it took us a while to come around

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:22.129
to the dangers of handling the substance.
Some of Curie’s possessions, such as her

00:05:22.129 --> 00:05:27.259
old cookbook, are still dangerous enough now
that they have to be stored in lead-lined

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:31.819
boxes. The French government is still cleaning
up traces of radioactive waste from the Radium

00:05:31.819 --> 00:05:36.599
craze to this day.
We didn’t realise the problem soon enough

00:05:36.599 --> 00:05:41.009
and it was used across Europe and America,
both in medicine and also for its glow in

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:47.219
the dark quality. A famous court case arose
around The Radium Girls – a group of women

00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:52.960
who had been painting watch faces with Radium
paint. They would lick the tips of their brushes

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.960
and it was a few years before they realised
they were poisoning themselves.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:02.939
Speaking of paint, when I say graffiti, most
people are going to think of street art like

00:06:02.939 --> 00:06:07.800
Banksy or JR, or maybe you’ll picture some
toilet scribble written with a Sharpie.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:13.520
But for as long as there has been bored people
and walls, we’ve had graffiti. And one of

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.189
the most widely spread doodles is ‘Kilroy
was here’.

00:06:17.189 --> 00:06:21.509
It’s difficult to pin down a real origin
for this, especially because the quote and

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:26.509
the image actually come from different places
but got combined at some point during World

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:30.699
War II, probably due to all the soldiers hanging
out and killing time together.

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:37.360
The image was seen as early as World War I,
known to Australians as Foo, and to the Brits

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.949
as Mr Chad. He’s ended up with plenty of
other names over the years, such as Herbie,

00:06:42.949 --> 00:06:47.610
Overby, Flywheel, Private Snoops, The Jeep
and Clem.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:53.559
The British Mr Chad would often appear with
a slogan saying “Wot, no tea?” or “Wot,

00:06:53.559 --> 00:06:58.740
no sugar?” as a complaint about rationing.
As for the image, it could have been taken

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:04.050
from lots of things. One popular idea is that
it comes from electrical circuits, since the

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:08.830
Australian version often had a plus and minus
symbol for eyes and resembled the diagram

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:14.219
for a sine wave, or even the omega; a symbol
for electrical resistance.

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:19.009
The phrase “Kilroy was here” is American
though, and is likely from a ship inspector

00:07:19.009 --> 00:07:24.919
called James J Kilroy who would sign the phrase
on parts of the ship to show that he’d checked

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:30.249
them. Since production during World War II
was so fast, often parts would not get painted

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:35.229
over, so navy personnel would find his phrase
scrawled in the most unlikely corners of the

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:38.840
ship and this is maybe how the legend was
born.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:43.809
It was so popular in the 40s and 50s that
it’s said to have been drawn on everything

00:07:43.809 --> 00:07:49.900
from the Great Wall of China to the Statue
of Liberty and, rumour has it, even the moon.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:55.539
It’s also popped up in plenty of books,
with a nod from Isaac Asimov, Robert A. Heinlein,

00:07:55.539 --> 00:08:00.779
Thomas Pynchon and even Bill Watterson, in
everyone’s favourite; Calvin and Hobbes.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:06.150
You’re at a party and what better way to
get things going than with a dance? It doesn’t

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.939
matter if you’ve got no rhythm or you’re
not wearing the right shoes because today’s

00:08:09.939 --> 00:08:12.339
dance just involves walking under a stick.

00:08:12.339 --> 00:08:17.449
That’s right, it’s the Limbo; the dance
that broke a thousand hips. It has everything

00:08:17.449 --> 00:08:22.199
you’d want from a dance; terrible music,
awkward poses and a high probability of horrific

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:29.909
injury. The strange thing is; this dance originated
in Trinidad and was originally performed at

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:35.090
wakes. Nothing says “you will be missed”
like rubbing your nipples against a flaming

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:35.760
stick.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:41.930
The 1957 film Fire Down Below is where the
dancer Julia Edward introduced Limbo to a

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:48.540
wider audience and Chubby Checker’s hit
single Limbo Rock in 1962 helped to popularise

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:51.330
it.
It’s not as common at parties these days,

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:56.210
it’s hard to limbo to electronic music I
guess, but as a competitive sport, it’s

00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:01.980
still going strong. The current world record
holder is Shemika Charles, a local Trinidadian

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:07.000
who’s keeping the tradition alive. In 2010
she danced her way into the Guinness world

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:14.330
record book, shimmying under a bar just 21.5
cm high; that’s less than the height of

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:19.520
the Guinness record book itself! She even
went all the way under a car.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.280
Limbo is part of a long list of dance crazes,
allowing drunk uncles at weddings all over

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.910
the world to look as ridiculous as possible
whilst trying to show up their in-laws.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:33.930
While most people can at least attempt to
dance; our next craze was popular because

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:38.690
of its innate difficulty.
The Rubik’s Cube was a multi-coloured puzzle

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:43.900
guaranteed to make you feel as dumb as a monkey
holding a smartphone. Invented in 1974 by

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:50.880
Ernő Rubik, a Hungarian sculptor and professor
of architecture, the cube is about 6x6 cm

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:56.340
and has a 3D cross in the middle with 26 plastic
cubes attached around it. The aim is to arrange

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:01.100
the cube so that each side is all one colour
and no, you’re not allowed to just peel

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.220
off the stickers.
Over 350 million Rubik’s Cubes have been

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:11.420
sold around the world and it is the best-selling
toy in history. It’s notoriously difficult

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:17.710
for most of us since there are a staggering
43 quintillion different combinations. But,

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:22.070
for a few fast-fingered brainboxes, it’s
a piece of cake and the world speed record

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:27.350
stands at just 4.90 seconds, by 14-year-old
Lucas Etter.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.870
Various other forms of the Rubik’s cube
have appeared over the years, with increasing

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:37.810
numbers of sizes and shapes; from a simple
four by four cube, up to a Yottaminx. Now,

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:44.210
a Yottaminx is a regular dodecahedron, meaning
it has twelve faces, all pentagons, which

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:53.680
divided into 15 by 15 pieces. It needs 2,943
parts to make it. If you do manage to solve

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:59.860
it, it opens up a much more important puzzle;
which is, what are doing with your life?

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:06.850
Our next craze was also a puzzle, of sorts,
but the pieces were people. In the late 50s,

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:11.570
phone booth stuffing became a challenge that
spread through colleges across North America,

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:19.330
Britain and South Africa and on 20th March
1959 a world record was set when 25 South

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:24.230
African men managed to cram their way into
a phone box, at the Durban YMCA.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:30.270
And this remains the record, despite the many
different attempts made and a variety of techniques

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:34.990
that students dreamed up, whilst doing their
best to avoid any actual work. A Canadian

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:40.360
school managed to get forty in a booth but
they had tipped it on its side and used one

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:42.860
that was already larger than average.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:48.230
So rules were set up, regarding the size and
position of the phone booth, as well as how

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:54.610
much of a person needed to be inside to count
towards the total. The Brits took it one step

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:59.940
further. Since we’re sticklers for doing
things properly; you had to make or receive

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:05.690
a call in order to complete the attempt.
“Yeah, Hi mum… just in a phone box with

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:09.380
39 other people, I think I might be late for
dinner”

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:13.660
If you want to try it for yourself, then the
sooner the better, for the mighty phone booth

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:20.610
is becoming extinct. In the U.S, the peak
number was 2.6 million in 1995 but that number

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:27.000
has plummeted and it’s now well under 500,000
and dropping every day. With the prevalence

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:32.540
of mobile phones, a phone booth is basically
a tiny, smelly museum with bad advertising

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:37.490
and some suspicious flyers.
If you were thinking about making a call in

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:42.620
Brazil, and leafing your way through the phone
book to look up a number, you might start

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:48.730
to notice a rather wonderful Brazilian craze
that is still going on to this day; giving

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:53.720
your children crazy names.
In some countries, such as Denmark, there

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:58.570
are very strict laws around naming a new-born.
There is a list of approved names, around

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:02.880
7000 in Denmark’s case, and the name must
correctly indicate the gender of the child.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:08.970
In Brazil though, it’s practically a competition
to see who can come up with the longest, obscurest

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:14.800
names, referencing everything from historical
figures (for example Francisco Lindon Johnson

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:23.360
Menezes da Luz Junior), also numbers (such
as Dix-Sept Rosado) or office equipment (like

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.880
Xerox Porfirio).
So what’s the reason for all of this? Well

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:33.080
it’s hard to say exactly. No doubt the rich
history of immigration and colonialism had

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:38.130
a big impact on the naming culture and there
has been a feeling of higher class attached

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:45.880
to some countries outside Brazil, such as
the US, so therefore names associated with

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:51.190
these countries probably hope to adopt part
of their perceived glamour. Although I’m

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:56.920
not sure Oleúde José Ribeiro, would agree.
This footballer’s first name is supposed

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:02.450
to be ‘Hollywood’ but the Brazillianisation
has lost a little something along the way.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:07.090
He’s not the only comically named player
either, he’s beaten by Credence Clearwater

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:11.000
Couto.
So what if Brazilian folk tire of being referred

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:17.970
to as Mao Tse Tund Lima de Moura or Oceano
Atlântico Linhares? Well you can legally

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:23.070
change it but the court system is very slow
and difficult and many people just choose

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:27.460
not to bother, embracing their parent’s
decision and buying an extra wide ID card.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.150
Brazil doesn’t have a monopoly on creative
nomenclature though. Frank Zappa, the American

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:29.730
rock genius, had four lovely children named;
Moon Unit, Ahmet, Dweezil and Diva Thin Muffin

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.420
Pigeen.
Our final fad is one close to my heart, because

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:36.660
I played it on many a sunny afternoon back
when I was a kid. It is the ferociously competitive

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:41.490
game called; Conkers.
Conkers are horse chestnuts, known as Buckeyes

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:46.590
in North America, and they usually start falling
from the tall trees in their green spiky shells,

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:51.100
around the middle of September. Unlike their
brothers, the sweet chestnut, which have a

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:56.570
much spikier shell and pointed seed, they
are not edible, in fact they are very mildly

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.860
poisonous.
But since they are found lying around outside

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:04.950
school yards, kids were smart enough to put
them to good use. The aim of conkers is to

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:10.410
smash your opponent’s conker. You drill
a small hole through the middle of the conker

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:15.840
and then attach a sting. You take it in turns
to strike the other person’s conker until

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.060
one of the conker cracks. It’s basically
British Pokémon; with conkers instead of

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:26.660
Poke Balls and cute furry animals.
There was actually a classic N64 game called

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:32.090
Conkers Bad Fur Day but it was platformer
about a dirty mouthed squirrel, rather than

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:38.350
a very niche conker battle simulator. Typing
BOVRILBULLETHOLE in the cheat menu here would

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:44.350
give you 50 lives, but cheating in actual
conkers required a lot more effort. Some of

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:49.620
the more popular methods were boiling them
in vinegar and then baking them, or painting

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:55.120
them with nail polish. But if you really want
to be king of the playground, World Conker

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:01.020
Champion, Charlie Bray claims that “the
best is to pass it through a pig. The conker

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:06.240
will harden by soaking in its stomach juices.
Then you search through the pig’s waste

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.890
to find the conker.”
Hey, sometimes you’ve got to get your hands

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:15.200
dirty if you want to be a real winner.
We roll our eyes at every stupid new fad that

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:21.820
comes along but we all know, deep down that
we’ve been a part of something just as ridiculous

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:28.990
before and probably will be again, it’s
what makes life interesting, right?

